in a lot of our examples you're going to  see data collaters popping up over and  over  they're used in both the pi torch and  the tensorflow workflows and maybe even  in jacks but no one really knows what's  happening in jaxx and we do have a  research team working on it though so  maybe they'll tell us soon but coming  back on topic what are data collaters  data collaters collate data that's not  that helpful but to be more specific  they put together a list of samples into  a single training mini batch  for some tasks the data collater can be  very straightforward  for example when you're doing sequence  classification  all you really need from your data  collater is that it pads your samples to  the same length and concatenates them  into a single tensor  but for other workflows data collaters  can be quite complex as they handle some  of the pre-processing needed for that  particular task  so when you want to use a data collator  for pi torch users you usually pass the  data collatera to your trainer object  in tensorflow it's a bit different the  easiest way to use a data collater is to  pass it to the 2tf dataset method of  your dataset and this will give you a  tensorflow tfdata.dataset  that you can then pass to model.fit  you'll see these approaches used in the  examples and notebooks throughout this  course  also note that all of our collaterals  take a return tensors argument you can  set this to pt to get pi torch tensors  tf to get tensorflow tensors or np to  get numpy arrays  for backward compatibility reasons the  default value is pt  so pi torch users don't even have to set  this argument most of the time and so as  a result they're often totally unaware  that this argument even exists  we can learn something from this which  is that the beneficiaries of privilege  are often the ones most blind to its  existence  but okay coming back let's see how  some specific data colliders work in  action  although again remember if none of the  built-in data collaterals do what you  need you can always write your own and  they're often quite short  so first we'll see the basic data  collaters these are  default data collater and data collater  with padding these are the ones you  should use if your labels are  straightforward and your data doesn't  need any special processing before being  ready for training  and note that because different models  have different padding tokens  data collater with padding will need  your model's tokenizer so it knows how  to pad sequences properly  the default data collator doesn't need a  tokenizer to work but it will as a  result throw an error unless all of your  sequences are the same length so you  should be aware of that  moving on though  a lot of the other data collaters aside  from the basic two are they're usually  designed to handle one specific task  and so i'm going to show you a couple  here these are data collater for token  classification and data collater for  seek to seek and the reason these tasks  need special collaters is because their  labels are variable in length  in token classification there's one  label for each token and so the length  of the labels is the length of the  sequence  while in seek to seek the labels are a  sequence of tokens that can be variable  length that can be very different from  the length of the input sequence  so in both of these cases we handle  collating that batch  by padding the labels as well  as you can see here in this example  so  inputs and labels will need to be padded  if we want to join  samples of variable length into the same  mini batch that's always the case across  all data collaters and that's exactly  what these data collators will do for us  in the you know for these particular  tasks  so there's one final data collector i  want to show you as well just in this  lecture and that's the data collater for  language modeling so it's very important  and it's firstly because language models  are just so foundational to do for  everything we do with nlp these days  but secondly because it has two modes  that do two very different things  so you choose which mode you want with  the mlm argument set it to true for  masked language modeling and set it to  false for causal language modeling  so collating data for causal language  modeling is actually quite  straightforward the model is just making  predictions for what token comes next  and so your labels are more or less just  a copy of your inputs and the collateral  will handle that and ensure that the  inputs and labels are padded correctly  when you set mlm to true though  you get a very quite different behavior  that's different from any other data  collater and that's because setting mlm  to true means masked language modeling  and that means the labels need to be  you know the inputs need to be masked  so what does that look like  so recall that in masked language  modeling the model is not predicting the  next word instead we randomly mask out  some tokens and the model predicts all  of them at once it tries to kind of fill  in the blanks for those masked tokens  but the process of random masking is  surprisingly complex  if we follow the protocol from the  original bert paper we need to replace  some tokens with a mask token some other  tokens with a random token and then we  keep a third set of tokens unchanged  this is not the lecture to go into you  know the specifics of that or why we do  it  you know you can always check out the  original bert paper if you're curious  it's well written it's easy to  understand  the main thing to know here is that it  can be a real pain and quite complex to  implement that yourself  but the data collater for language  modelling will do it for you when you  set mlm to true and that's an example of  the more intricate uh pre-processing  that some of our data collaterals do  and that's it  so this covers the most commonly used  data colliders and the tasks they're  used for and hopefully now you'll know  when to use data colliders and which one  to choose  when you have for your specific task
 

